  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1211 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nLows Tuesday night will once again remain very mild for this time\nof year with mid to upper 70s likely areawide as the moist humid \nair remains. By Wednesday afternoon, we should see highs again \nreach the low to mid 90s along with plenty of moisture thus \npushing heat indices into the 100-106 degree range. Along with the\nreturn of our typical seabreeze we should see more chances for \nshowers and storms (30-40%) form across the Coastal Plains and \nperhaps as far north as the I-35 Corridor and into the Hill \ncountry.\n\nBy late week we should begin to see the pattern change once more \nas our subtropical ridge moves off to the east and the upper \ntrough moves over the Rockies and into the Coastal Plains. This in\nturn drives a cold front down through our area. Global models \nhave done fairly poorly so far on the timing and strength of this \nfront so it remains to be seen just how much cooler we get behind \nit and how much moisture we can wring out of it. As of right now \neach model run keeps coming in drier for the I-35 corridor and \neastern half of our area so have went with a blend of temps and \npops until we get more model consistence on how this pattern \nunfolds. One thing that has started to happen is models are \nstarting to hone in on moisture becoming trapped ahead of the \nfront across the Southern Edwards Plateau and Rio Grande Plains \nareas. This would increase the potential for the possibility of \nlocally heavy rainfall Thursday and Friday. As such WPC has begun \nto highlight mostly Val Verde County in a level 2 of 4 (slight) \nrisk for excessive rainfall and a level 1 of 4 (marginal) risk for\nareas just west of highway 281 for day 4 (Thursday). Some models \nthen bring in significantly cooler air following this front while \nothers have it not as cool. Regardless what happens we could be \nlooking at highs only reaching the 70s to low 80s Friday with \nhighs remaining in the 60s and 70s for the weekend. Continue to \ncheck back in as the forecast becomes clearer in the coming days.",
